```java
@Service
class OrderService {
    // Anti-pattern: the service news up its own dependency
    private final OrderRepository repo = new OrderRepository();

    void placeOrder(Order o) {
        repo.save(o); // tightly coupled, impossible to mock in a test
    }
}
```

```java
@Service
class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repo;

    // Spring injects the repository through the constructor
    OrderService(OrderRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }

    void placeOrder(Order o) {
        repo.save(o); // loosely coupled, easy to mock in a test
    }
}
```
The service no longer cares how the repository is built; it just declares the need and uses whatever Spring delivers.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repository;
    private final NotificationService notifier;

    // Spring injects both dependencies through the constructor
    public OrderService(OrderRepository repository,
                        NotificationService notifier) {
        this.repository = repository;
        this.notifier = notifier;
    }

    public Order createOrder(OrderRequest request) {
        var order = new Order(request.customerId(), request.items());
        repository.save(order);           // injected dependency, no 'new'
        notifier.sendConfirmation(order); // injected dependency
        return order;
    }
}

@Repository
public class OrderRepository {
    public void save(Order order) {
        // database persistence logic
    }
}
```
The `final` fields make each dependency mandatory and immutable once the bean is built.

