DI keeps the dependency visible and mockable; Service Locator hides it behind a global lookup.

```java
interface Notifier { void send(String msg); }

@Component class EmailNotifier implements Notifier { public void send(String m) {} }
@Component class SmsNotifier   implements Notifier { public void send(String m) {} }

@Service
class AlertService {
    // Two Notifier beans exist -> NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException at startup
    AlertService(Notifier notifier) { }
}
```

```java
@Component @Primary            // the default Notifier when none is named
class EmailNotifier implements Notifier { public void send(String m) {} }

@Component("smsNotifier")
class SmsNotifier implements Notifier { public void send(String m) {} }

@Service
class AlertService {
    // No qualifier -> Spring injects the @Primary EmailNotifier
    AlertService(Notifier notifier) { }
}

@Service
class OtpService {
    private final Notifier notifier;
    // @Qualifier overrides @Primary -> injects the SMS bean
    OtpService(@Qualifier("smsNotifier") Notifier notifier) {
        this.notifier = notifier;
    }
}
```
For refactor-safe selection, replace the string name with a custom qualifier annotation such as `@Sms`.

